.TH "jeopardy\-protocol" "7" "October 5 2026" "jeopardy 0.1.0" "CIC Miscellaneous Manual"
.SH NAME
.PP
jeopardy\-protocol \(en wire protocol between Jeopardy clients and
server
.SH DESCRIPTION
Clients and \f[B]jeopardy\-server\f[R](1) exchange newline\-delimited
JSON over a UNIX or TCP stream socket.
Each line is one JSON object.
A line longer than 16 KiB closes the connection.
.PP
The client sends requests.
The server answers with a reply to the sender only when the request
fails or is a \f[B]join\f[R], and then sends the new game state to every
connected client.
Requests that change nothing, such as an early buzz, still produce a new
state.
.SH REQUESTS
Every request is an object with an \f[B]op\f[R] field and the arguments
listed below.
A message with an unknown \f[B]op\f[R], a missing argument or an
argument of the wrong type gets an \f[B]error\f[R] reply.
.PP
Apart from \f[B]join\f[R], requests are accepted only after a successful
join.
The server enforces who may send each one, and when.
The \f[I]chooser\f[R] is the player who has control of the board:
.PP
