.TE
.PP
\f[B]cat\f[R], \f[B]idx\f[R], \f[B]amount\f[R] and \f[B]delta\f[R] are
integers.
\f[B]correct\f[R] is a boolean.
The other arguments are strings.
\f[B]target\f[R] is a player id.
Names are 1 to 20 printable characters, and answers longer than 200
characters are cut.
.PP
\f[B]role\f[R] is \f[B]host\f[R], \f[B]player\f[R] or
\f[B]spectator\f[R].
There is one host per game, and names are unique, ignoring case.
A valid \f[B]token\f[R] from an earlier join takes the old seat back and
overrides \f[B]name\f[R] and \f[B]role\f[R].
.SH REPLIES
.TP
\f[B]welcome\f[R]
Reply to a successful join:
\f[CR]{\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqwelcome\(dq,\(dqtoken\(dq:T,\(dqid\(dq:P}\f[R].
\f[B]token\f[R] is secret and proves identity on reconnect.
\f[B]id\f[R] is the public player id used as \f[B]target\f[R].
Spectators get null for both.
.TP
\f[B]error\f[R]
\f[CR]{\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqerror\(dq,\(dqmessage\(dq:M}\f[R].
The request had no effect.
.TP
\f[B]state\f[R]
\f[CR]{\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqstate\(dq,\(dqstate\(dq:S,\(dqsets\(dq:[...]}\f[R],
sent after every change and after joining.
\f[B]sets\f[R] lists the question sets the host can start.
.SH STATE
\f[B]state\f[R] holds the whole game, as seen by one viewer: the phase,
title, board, players and scores, who is in control, who has the floor,
the last 50 log lines, and \f[B]you\f[R], the viewer\(cqs own id (null
for spectators).
.PP
